 <h2 id="overview">Purpose and learning objectives</h2>
 <p>The joint publication calls close air support perhaps the most detailed and continuous integration task the joint force performs, because the air attack has to be synchronized in time, space and purpose with troops who may be very near the target. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-1')}</a> Most of that integration is done with a small set of standard formats and calls, so that an aircrew from any service can work with a controller from any other. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-1')}</a> This module teaches those formats from the cockpit's side.</p>
 <p>By the end you should be able to: say who may control a CAS attack and what a <Term>FAC(A)</Term> can take over; choose between <Term>Type 1 control</Term>, <Term>Type 2 control</Term> and <Term>Type 3 control</Term>, and between <Term>BOT</Term> and <Term>BOC</Term>; give a <Term>CAS check-in</Term> in order; copy a <Term>9-line</Term> and say what each line means and its units; give the <Term>mandatory readback</Term>; explain <Term>correlation</Term>, a <Term>talk-on</Term> and the laser and infrared marking calls; use <Term>CLEARED HOT</Term>, <Term>CLEARED TO ENGAGE</Term>, <Term>CONTINUE</Term>, <Term>CONTINUE DRY</Term> and <Term>ABORT</Term> correctly; explain <Term>danger close</Term>; and copy a helicopter <Term>5-line</Term>.</p>
 <p class="note">This is a reference, not a qualification. Only a certified and qualified <Term>JTAC</Term> or <Term>FAC(A)</Term> is authorized to control an attack. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-2')}</a> How to fly the delivery itself, the dive, the release and the recovery, is in <a href={wd}>Weapons Delivery</a>; how to talk on the radio, including readbacks and the brevity words, is in <a href={radio}>Radio Procedures</a>. The worked brief in Figure 1 is an invented example written in the publication's format: its call signs, places and numbers are made up. The publication quoted is the 2014 edition; theater special instructions (SPINS), your unit's procedures and the current edition govern.</p>

 <h2 id="what">What close air support is</h2>
 <p><Term>CAS</Term> is air action by fixed- and rotary-wing aircraft against hostile targets that are in close proximity to friendly forces and that requires detailed integration of each air mission with the fire and movement of those forces. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-1')}</a> "Close" is not a distance: it is the need for that detailed integration, because of proximity, fires or movement, that makes an attack CAS. CAS may destroy, disrupt, suppress, fix, harass, neutralize or delay enemy forces. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-2')}</a></p>
 <p>The ground commanders are the ultimate authority for all supporting fires in their area: the lowest-level ground commander employs the CAS assets unless a higher commander keeps that authority, and decides the target priority, the effects and the timing. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-3')}</a> Aircrews keep the primary responsibility for weaponeering recommendations and their own employment tactics, while the <Term>JTAC</Term> or <Term>FAC(A)</Term> concentrates on the effects on the target. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-4')}</a></p>
 <p>The conditions for effective CAS, the publication says, are well-trained people, effective planning and integration, effective command and control, air superiority (especially the suppression of enemy air defenses), target marking or acquisition, streamlined and flexible procedures, and the right ordnance. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-6')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="who">Who controls what</h2>
 <p><Term>Terminal attack control</Term> is the authority to control the maneuver of attacking aircraft and to grant them weapons release clearance. A certified and qualified <Term>JTAC</Term> or <Term>FAC(A)</Term> is recognized across the Department of Defense as able and authorized to perform it, whichever service trained them. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-2')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>The <Term>JTAC</Term></strong> is a qualified (certified) service member who, from a forward position, directs the action of combat aircraft engaged in close air support. <a href="#source-jp">{j('GL-11')}</a> The supported commander delegates weapons release authority to the JTAC for specific engagements. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-40')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>The <Term>FAC(A)</Term></strong> is a specifically trained and qualified aviator, normally an airborne extension of the <Term>TACP</Term>, who controls CAS from the air. <a href="#source-jp">{j('GL-10')}</a> A FAC(A) should say "FAC(A) capable" or "qualified" at check-in. It can provide terminal attack control under all three types, relay radio calls, reconnoiter and recommend weapons, call for artillery, deconflict aircraft and surface fires, mark and talk aircraft on, and pass battle damage assessment. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-55 to V-57')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>A <Term>JFO</Term></strong> can request, adjust and control surface-to-surface fires and provide targeting information in support of Type 2 and 3 control by a JTAC or FAC(A). <a href="#source-jp">{j('GL-11')}</a> Anyone else doing <Term>terminal guidance operations</Term>, such as lasing a target or generating a coordinate, has no authority to control or clear aircraft unless qualified as a JTAC or FAC(A). <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-2')}</a></li>
 </ul>
 <p>Before an aircraft reaches the controller, an air control agency usually briefs it from the request: the Air Force's <Term>ASOC</Term>, the Marine Corps' <Term>DASC</Term>, a Marine or Navy tactical air control center, or a <Term>TAC(A)</Term>. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-3')}</a> <a href="#source-jp">{j('GL-8 to GL-9, GL-13')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>Splitting the job.</strong> When a JTAC and a FAC(A) work together they divide four duties, named by brevity words that each must echo back: <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-57 to V-58')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>BRIEF</strong>: the FAC(A) passes area updates, CAS briefs and battle damage assessment to the aircraft and copies their check-ins.</li>
  <li><strong>STACK</strong>: the FAC(A) deconflicts all the CAS aircraft from each other and from surface fires, including holding areas, ingress and egress.</li>
  <li><strong>MARK</strong>: the FAC(A) marks targets; a talk-on counts as a mark. Marking autonomously, it still calls <Term>IN</Term> before releasing so the JTAC can abort it.</li>
  <li><strong>CONTROL</strong>: the FAC(A) gives final release authority, if the JTAC delegates it. As always, the JTAC keeps abort authority.</li>
 </ul>
 <p>So a JTAC might say "Bengal 61 has stack and mark, Titus maintains brief and control", or hand a FAC(A) everything: "your brief, stack, mark, and control". Whoever holds final release authority must be stated clearly. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-57 to V-58')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="control">Types of control</h2>
 <p>The type of control tells everyone how the controller intends to reduce the risk of each attack. It is chosen from the commander's tactical risk assessment and passed in the game plan, before the brief. No level of risk is attached to a type: GPS and digital targeting under Type 2 may reduce risk more than Type 1, and none of the three is ordnance-specific. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-42 to III-43')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Types of terminal attack control" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>Types of terminal attack control <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-2, III-43 to III-47')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">The controller</th><th scope="col">Attacks cleared</th><th scope="col">Clearance</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>{CONTROLS.map((c) => <tr><th scope="row">Type {c.id}</th><td>{c.needs}</td><td>{c.attacks}</td><td>{c.clearance}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p><strong><Term>Type 1 control</Term></strong> is for when the best way to protect friendly troops is for the controller to watch each aircraft and predict its weapon's path from its geometry. The controller withholds clearance until the aircraft has finished maneuvering on the target, and may restrict weapons that can fly well off the aircraft's nose. Because a GPS- or INS-guided weapon flies to its coordinates whatever the aircraft's geometry, such weapons should not be controlled under Type 1. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-43')}</a></p>
 <p><strong><Term>Type 2 control</Term></strong> is for when the controller still controls each attack but cannot see the aircraft at release, or cannot see the target and uses accurate real-time targeting data from another asset. The publication's examples are troops in contact, night, bad weather and high-altitude or standoff weapons. The controller should still try to watch the aircraft. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-44 to III-45')}</a></p>
 <p><strong><Term>Type 3 control</Term></strong> clears multiple attacks within one engagement, inside restrictions the controller sets, such as a time, a boundary, headings or a target set. After the readback the controller says <Term>CLEARED TO ENGAGE</Term>; the aircrew calls "COMMENCING ENGAGEMENT" before its first release and "ENGAGEMENT COMPLETE" at the end. The controller monitors by every means available and keeps abort authority. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-46')}</a></p>
 <p>For Types 1 and 2 the steps are the same until the end: the controller acquires the target, passes the game plan and brief; the aircrew checks that the location falls where expected and reads back; the controller correlates; the aircrew calls <Term>IN</Term>; and the controller answers <Term>CLEARED HOT</Term>, <Term>CONTINUE DRY</Term> or <Term>ABORT</Term>. Under Type 1 the controller must see the aircraft and judge its geometry before answering. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-44 to III-45')}</a> Two types can be in effect at once for different flights, and any change of type has to be made before the IN call, or before COMMENCING ENGAGEMENT under Type 3; after it, the controller aborts and rebriefs. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-43, III-50')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="method">Methods of attack</h2>
 <p>The method of attack is separate from the type of control and is an agreement about one thing: whether the aircraft must acquire the target. Any type of control can go with either method. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-47')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong><Term>BOT</Term> (bomb on target)</strong>: the aircrew must acquire the target or the intended aimpoint, <Term>TALLY</Term>, <Term>CONTACT</Term> or <Term>CAPTURED</Term>. The coordinates only need to cue the aircrew and support fires approval. Examples: a laser-guided bomb lased by the delivering aircraft, unguided bombs under the weather with the aircrew TALLY, an attack corrected from a mark, and rockets and guns on a positively identified target. Only the lead is required to read back. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-48 to III-49')}</a></li>
  <li><strong><Term>BOC</Term> (bomb on coordinate)</strong>: the aircraft employs on the coordinates in the brief and is never required to see the target or the mark. The coordinates must be precise enough for the effect wanted and for fires approval, the aircrew does not change them once read back, and every aircraft delivering ordnance reads back from its weapon or system. Examples: an inertially aided weapon on a fixed coordinate, a laser-guided weapon guided by someone outside the flight, and unguided bombs from medium or high altitude above an overcast. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-47 to III-48')}</a></li>
 </ul>
 <p>Misusing the two, the publication warns, causes confusion, slows the attack and can lead to fratricide. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-48')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="clearance">Clearance calls</h2>
 <p>Weapons release authority lets the controller give these calls, and only these: <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-40 to III-41')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong><Term>ABORT</Term></strong>: cease the attack before weapons release, in any type of control.</li>
  <li><strong><Term>CLEARED HOT</Term></strong>: Types 1 and 2, clearance to release on this pass. To clear a whole flight the controller adds "flight" after the call sign: "Ragin' 41, flight, CLEARED HOT".</li>
  <li><strong><Term>CONTINUE</Term></strong>: continue the attack profile; release is not yet granted.</li>
  <li><strong><Term>CONTINUE DRY</Term></strong>: continue the pass without releasing, as in training or a show of force; under Type 3 it is "Type 3, CONTINUE DRY".</li>
  <li><strong><Term>CLEARED TO ENGAGE</Term></strong>: Type 3 clearance to attack within the controller's parameters.</li>
 </ul>
 <p class="note">The word CLEARED is used only when ordnance is actually to be delivered, to keep bombs from coming off on dry passes, and nonstandard calls are always avoided. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-40')}</a></p>

