 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <details><summary>What must you read back from a 9-line?</summary><p>Line 4 (target elevation), line 6 (target location) and every restriction, which always includes final attack headings, ACAs, danger close and the TOT. The controller may ask for more.</p></details>
 <details><summary>The JTAC wants to drop a GPS-guided bomb on a coordinate. Can it be controlled as Type 1?</summary><p>It should not be: the aircraft's geometry does not show where a GPS or INS weapon will fly, which is the point of Type 1.</p></details>
 <details><summary>What is the difference between the type of control and the method of attack?</summary><p>The type of control is how the controller reduces the risk and how many attacks it clears; the method of attack is whether the aircraft must acquire the target (BOT) or employs on the coordinates (BOC). Any type goes with either method.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Your laser spot tracker shows a spot. Are you CAPTURED?</summary><p>Not necessarily. Call SPOT; the controller then confirms what is under your crosshairs, because spot jitter or spillover can put the spot slightly off the target.</p></details>
 <details><summary>A ground controller is marking the target with an IR pointer. What must you call before you can be cleared?</summary><p>VISUAL for the friendly position and TALLY or CONTACT SPARKLE, on every pass.</p></details>
 <details><summary>What makes an attack danger close, and who accepts the risk?</summary><p>Ordnance delivered inside the 0.1 percent probability-of-incapacitation distance. The supported commander accepts it by passing their initials through the controller; danger close is a restriction and is read back.</p></details>
 <details><summary>A FAC(A) has been given control. Who can still abort you?</summary><p>The JTAC, who always keeps abort authority; and anyone can call an abort at any time they think it necessary.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Why are attack headings planned parallel to the FLOT where possible?</summary><p>Because misses fall long or short along the bomb-fall line, so a heading parallel to the friendly line keeps them from landing on friendly troops.</p></details>

 <h2 id="sources">Sources and applicability</h2>
 <h3>Military source</h3>
 <p id="source-jp"><strong>[1] US Joint Chiefs of Staff, JP 3-09.3, Close Air Support, 25 November 2014.</strong> <a href="https://irp.fas.org/doddir/dod/jp3_09_3.pdf">Read on the Federation of American Scientists' copy</a>, 1 November 2026, by printed page: chapter I (introduction and fundamentals), pp. I-1 to I-11; troops in contact, risk-estimate distance and danger close, pp. III-36 to III-37; clearance calls and post-launch abort, pp. III-40 to III-42; types of control and methods of attack, pp. III-42 to III-50; threats, pp. III-51 to III-52; marking, laser and infrared brevity and friendly marking, pp. III-30 and III-75 to III-81; aircraft tactics and the rotary-wing 5-line, pp. III-84 to III-97; execution, pp. V-1 to V-58; glossary, pp. GL-8 to GL-14. The joint publication for all four services. A later edition exists on the Joint Chiefs' own site, which could not be read from the authoring environment, so some wording here may have changed.</p>
 <p>Page-specific excerpts are recorded in the repository review evidence: <a href="https://github.com/burnzoire/oversweep/blob/main/docs/reviews/close-air-support-evidence.md">close air support evidence</a>.</p>
 <p>Figure 1 is an original drawing of an invented example, not a reproduction of any publication's figure.</p>
 <p class="note">Applicability: written for aircrew of every service who fly close air support, fixed- or rotary-wing. Everything here is from the joint publication, including how it describes Marine Corps attack helicopter CAS and Army close combat attack; the Army's own aviation manual and the Navy's CAS training instructions could not be read for this revision, so no service-specific rules beyond the joint publication's are included. Theater SPINS, your unit's procedures and the current edition govern. This revision is Drafted pending independent source-backed review.</p>
